#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130075} :activity/id #uuid "58849859-2309-5910-bd3d-67823d7df3a7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049003 :topic/id #uuid "d28d3589-1bfc-578d-a6de-3fced37e42ca" :topic/math-academy-id 1996} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130400 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the double integral  \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 2(x+y)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite region bounded by the lines $x=0, \\, y=1, \\, y=2, \\, y=3-x,$ and $y=x.$ How do we proceed?\n\nTo evaluate an integral like this, we carry out the following steps: \n\n**Step 1.** Sketch the region $D$. This allows us to determine the precise set-up of our integral. \n\nIn this example, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0a/0a90a4f377327f5293308f1e08ac6596f1c27b9bd47a614d6d7cb1c5dbfec33c.png)\n\n**Step 2.** Split (if necessary) the region $D$ into several type I or II regions.\n\nFrom the diagram above, we see that our region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions $D_1$ and $D_2,$ as follows.\n\n![](images/a9/a93e1a0a0a7f9c104cb5d380b2a7ff98d6712ef0d2d67b74130da5dcf1701443.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq 1, \\quad 1 \\leq y \\leq 2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2= \\{(x,y)\\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3/2,  \\quad  x \\leq y \\leq 3-x\\}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Set up and evaluate the integral using the additivity property of double integrals. We recall the following fact:\n\n*If $D$ is the union of two sub-regions, $D_1, D_2$, that don't overlap then,*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(x+y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}{\\int }_{x}^{3-x}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce229625-0660-5682-9e5a-3850f8ce33e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4358 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "677b9db4-978c-5d9a-9a29-29a0b08eb60b" :step/math-academy-id 26555 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129011 :question/id #uuid "b4697448-d5f7-59ae-86a1-689ae00ebb73" :question/math-academy-id "e-7197"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa355fd-5d73-5cce-9c76-1edafae19751" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351419}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Partitioned Region"} :step/id #uuid "2f89bd90-2162-53d4-845f-4123449e769b" :step/math-academy-id 26556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129012 :question/id #uuid "9a106b26-c6fe-5e4b-ae43-9cc02ddb67c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7198"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "264eb5c1-fb55-5ed2-bc46-5548555bc98c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475923}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral Over a Union of Type I Regions"} :step/id